---
// Astro returns BASE_URL without a trailing slash at the root but with one
// under a subpath; normalise so `${base}x` is never `/cyber-cvx`.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Disclosure from '../components/Disclosure.astro';
import { teamBrainSystem, factoriumSystem } from '../data/systems';
import { teamBrain } from '../data/team-brain';
import { repos, public_output } from '../data/work';
import { recentWork } from '../data/hive';
---
<Base
  title="Systems — Michal Malček"
  description="Two AI systems, the apparatus around them, what went wrong, and the code that is public."
  back={{ href: `${base}`, label: 'Back to overview' }}
>
  <header class="pt-10 pb-8 border-b border-line">
    <div class="eyebrow mb-3">What was built</div>
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(28px,4vw,40px)] font-semibold tracking-tight m-0 mb-3">
      <span class="glow-name">Systems</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="text-fg-dim max-w-[680px] m-0">
      One production system delivered and handed over, one running daily, the apparatus that grew
      around them, and the failures that shaped both. Every figure on this page comes from a store
      or a git history rather than from memory.
    </p>
  </header>

  <!-- ── Team Brain ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <Section id="team-brain" title="Team Brain" note="built · deployed · handed over">
    <p class="text-fg-dim max-w-[720px] mt-0 mb-5">
      {teamBrainSystem.tagline}. Idea at the end of January 2026, in daily use by February,
      handed over clean when my role ended — on <b class="text-fg">one 8&nbsp;GB VPS</b> that also
      carried everything else.
    </p>

    <div class="card p-5 mb-3">
      <div class="eyebrow mb-3">What it did, in the order you met it</div>
      <ol class="list-none p-0 m-0 flex flex-col gap-2.5">
        {teamBrainSystem.what.map((w, i) => (
          <li class="flex gap-3 text-[13.5px] text-fg-dim">
            <span class="lit-forge text-[13px] font-semibold shrink-0 w-4">{i + 1}</span>
            <span>{w}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="card p-5 mb-3">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap mb-1">
        <div class="eyebrow">{teamBrainSystem.dataLayer.lead}</div>
        <span class="text-[11px] text-fg-ghost">the part that made it more than a chat wrapper</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-fg-dim mt-2 mb-4">{teamBrainSystem.dataLayer.body}</p>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2.5">
        {teamBrainSystem.dataLayer.sources.map((src) => (
          <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-[132px_1fr] gap-1 sm:gap-4 pb-2.5 border-b border-line/40 last:border-b-0 last:pb-0">
            <b class="text-[13px] text-synapse">{src.name}</b>
            <span class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-dim">{src.detail}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card p-5 mb-3">
      <div class="eyebrow mb-4">Decisions worth defending</div>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-4">
        {teamBrainSystem.decisions.map((d) => (
          <div>
            <b class="text-[13px] text-fg block mb-1 leading-snug">{d.head}</b>
            <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-dim m-0">{d.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-[1.3fr_1fr] gap-3">
      <div class="card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-4">
          <div class="eyebrow">Measured in production</div>
          <span class="text-[11px] text-fg-ghost">{teamBrainSystem.measured.period}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mb-4">
          {[
            [`${teamBrainSystem.measured.toolSuccessPct}%`, `success over ${teamBrainSystem.measured.toolCalls.toLocaleString('en-US')} tool calls`],
            [teamBrainSystem.measured.messages.toLocaleString('en-US'), `messages · ${teamBrainSystem.measured.conversations} conversations`],
            [teamBrainSystem.measured.agentRuns.toLocaleString('en-US'), 'agent runs'],
            ['~49M', 'tokens processed'],
          ].map(([v, l]) => (
            <div>
              <div class="text-[19px] font-semibold lit-forge leading-none">{v}</div>
              <div class="text-[10.5px] text-fg-ghost leading-snug mt-1.5">{l}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="text-[12px] text-fg-ghost m-0 pt-3 border-t border-line/60">
          <b class="text-fg-dim">93.9% across 1,352 real calls</b> is the figure I would defend:
          not a benchmark, but non-engineers using external APIs through a model for four months.
          The failures clustered where you would expect, and knowing <i>which</i> tools degrade is
          only possible because every call was logged with its duration and outcome from week one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-3">
          <div class="eyebrow">Agents</div>
          <span class="text-[13px] text-forge font-semibold tabular-nums">
            {teamBrainSystem.agents.written} → {teamBrainSystem.agents.live}
          </span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-dim m-0 mb-3">{teamBrainSystem.agents.lesson}</p>
        <div class="text-[11px] text-fg-ghost pt-3 border-t border-line/60">
          {teamBrainSystem.planNodes} plan nodes carry the full history of this system — every
          decision, every run, every finding, in the same graph as the code.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5 mt-4">
      {teamBrainSystem.stack.map((t) => <span class="chip">{t}</span>)}
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ── Factorium ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <Section id="factorium" title="Factorium" note="running daily · pre-release">
    <p class="text-fg-dim max-w-[720px] mt-0 mb-5">
      {factoriumSystem.tagline}. Everything runs on <b class="text-fg">one Apple M4 with
      24&nbsp;GB and no cloud inference</b> — a ceiling that decided most of what follows.
    </p>

    <div class="card p-5 mb-3">
      <div class="eyebrow mb-3">What it is</div>
      <ol class="list-none p-0 m-0 flex flex-col gap-2.5">
        {factoriumSystem.what.map((w, i) => (
          <li class="flex gap-3 text-[13.5px] text-fg-dim">
            <span class="lit-cyan text-[13px] font-semibold shrink-0 w-4">{i + 1}</span>
            <span>{w}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mb-3">
      {factoriumSystem.pillars.map((p) => (
        <div class="card p-5">
          <b class="text-[13.5px] text-fg block mb-1.5 leading-snug">{p.head}</b>
          <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-dim m-0">{p.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
      {factoriumSystem.stack.map((t) => <span class="chip">{t}</span>)}
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ── The working layer ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <Section
    id="apparatus"
    title="The apparatus, and what it cost to get right"
    lede="The part of this work that rarely gets written down: the tooling you build around a model, and how much of it turns out to be waste. Both counts below come from git history, not from memory."
  >
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mb-4">
      <div class="card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-3">
          <div class="eyebrow">Agents · Team Brain</div>
          <span class="text-[13px] text-forge font-semibold tabular-nums">{teamBrain.agentsWritten} → {teamBrain.agentsLive}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[13.5px] text-fg-dim m-0 mb-3">
          I first built what I thought agentic meant: one specialised agent per task. It reached{' '}
          <b class="text-fg">{teamBrain.agentsWritten} definitions</b> and became unusable — nobody
          could tell which to reach for. The names were accurate and useless: you cannot choose
          between two analytics agents unless you already know the answer.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[13px] text-fg-ghost m-0">
          The redesign collapsed them into <b class="text-fg-dim">six specialists</b>, each
          absorbing the narrow agents it replaced as loadable context rather than a top-level
          choice. <b class="text-fg-dim">A capability taxonomy is a UX decision, not an
          architecture decision</b> — the right number is what a person can hold in their head
          while typing a question.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card p-5">
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-3">
          <div class="eyebrow">My own instruction set</div>
          <span class="text-[13px] text-forge font-semibold tabular-nums">18 → 7</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[13.5px] text-fg-dim m-0 mb-3">
          An audit found that two of my working instructions had been written for an entirely
          different product and never adapted; one assumed a framework this codebase does not use;
          one described a database schema we had migrated away from; one referenced four expert
          personas that were never built. All had been loading for months.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[13px] text-fg-ghost m-0">
          <b class="text-fg-dim">Instructions rot silently and confidently.</b> Code that references
          a deleted module fails to compile; prose that references a deleted concept keeps sounding
          authoritative. The fix was binding each instruction to a concrete tool call, so a stale
          one breaks instead of merely misleading.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card p-5 mb-4">
      <div class="eyebrow mb-4">Tool surface: two models, two very different budgets</div>
      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <div>
          <div class="text-[12px] text-fg-ghost mb-2">Large cloud model</div>
          <div class="text-[24px] font-semibold text-neural-text leading-none mb-2">full surface</div>
          <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-dim m-0">Context is cheap relative to capability, so
            curation would cost more than it saves.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="text-[12px] text-fg-ghost mb-2">Local 12B model</div>
          <div class="text-[24px] font-semibold text-synapse leading-none mb-2">22 of 60</div>
          <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-dim m-0">Curated per turn, the rest reachable through a
            search tool. Past roughly 15–20 always-visible schemas, a small model's tool selection
            degrades sharply.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-ghost mt-4 mb-0 pt-3 border-t border-line/60">
        Voice gets a third and smaller surface: read-only. Spoken interaction has no confirmation
        step, so nothing that writes is reachable that way — a blast-radius decision, not a
        capability one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card p-5 mb-4">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap mb-1">
        <div class="eyebrow">The last twelve commits</div>
        <span class="text-[11px] text-fg-ghost">read from git, not written here</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-dim mt-2 mb-4">
        Included because a commit history is the honest version of a CV: it is written while the
        work happens, by someone who does not yet know how it turns out. These are unedited.
      </p>
      <ol class="list-none p-0 m-0 flex flex-col">
        {recentWork.map((c) => (
          <li class="grid grid-cols-[86px_1fr] gap-3 py-[7px] border-b border-line/40 last:border-b-0 items-baseline">
            <span class="text-[11.5px] text-fg-ghost tabular-nums">{c.date}</span>
            <span class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-dim">{c.subject}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="card p-5">
      <div class="eyebrow mb-3">On protocol layers — why most of ours were removed</div>
      <p class="text-[13.5px] text-fg-dim m-0 mb-3">
        We ran two browser integrations through the standard tool protocol. Measured usage over the
        period: <b class="text-fg">1,548 calls to one, approximately zero to the other</b>. The
        second was not adding capability, it was adding a decision — <em>which one?</em> — at every
        step. Removed. Everything the hive itself does is served natively over plain HTTP instead,
        which puts one less layer between a call and its failure mode.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-fg-ghost m-0">
        <b class="text-fg-dim">The rule I would defend:</b> a protocol layer earns its place when it
        lets you use somebody else's implementation. When you own both sides of the interface, it is
        indirection with a specification attached. We keep it for fetching external content and
        driving a browser — genuinely external things, where someone else's implementation beats
        one we would write.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ── What went wrong ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <Section
    id="wrong"
    title="What went wrong"
    lede="These cost real time and are written down because the corrections are the part worth reading. Each one is a stored record, not a recollection."
  >
    <Disclosure summary="Using an embedding model outside its contract" tag="weeks">
      <p>nomic-embed requires asymmetric prefixes on queries and documents. Served through
      llama.cpp, nothing adds them, so everything was embedded symmetrically. Symptom: short
      meaningless text outranked long relevant text.</p>
      <p>The diagnosis method has since caught every similar defect — <em>embed a stored document
      both ways and compare each against its own stored vector.</em> Whichever scores 1.0000 tells
      you how it was really indexed.</p>
      <p class="text-forge">Cost: 10,676 records re-embedded. And the first repair script read only
      the body field while the writer had embedded title + body together — 95% of records would
      have silently lost their titles from their vectors. Nothing would have errored.</p>
    </Disclosure>

    <Disclosure summary="A migration that left its satellites behind" tag="days of silent loss">
      <p>Moving to a 1024-dimension embedding model updated the server and the main paths, but not
      eight standalone ingest scripts. The code indexer failed on every write for days, so the code
      graph ingested nothing. The failure was loud in a log nobody was reading.</p>
      <p>Root cause found later: the model and port live in the service's launchd plist, not in any
      shell, so every hand-run script fell back to the old model. Fixed twice at the symptom before
      being fixed at the source.</p>
    </Disclosure>

    <Disclosure summary="Adding a speech-to-text model, then removing it">
      <p>Voice transcription was poor, so I downloaded Whisper and benchmarked it. It won — on
      synthetic clips. On real microphone recordings the multimodal model transcribed correctly and
      the premise collapsed. Synthetic fixtures had misled me four separate times by then.</p>
      <p>The actual defect was the prompt: told to "transcribe this", the model answered the
      question it heard instead of writing it down. Recast as a transcription <em>service</em>, it
      returned exact text including filler words. No new model, no extra gigabytes.</p>
    </Disclosure>

    <Disclosure summary="The pattern behind 35 of 46 recorded mistakes" tag="the real one">
      <p>A check comes back empty and I read the emptiness as an answer. A filter eats an error. A
      key I read does not exist, so the value is zero, so I conclude the tool is broken. A directory
      listing fails on a permission error I do not read, so I report the folder as empty.</p>
      <p>You cannot learn this from a book, because a book cannot show you your own reflex. You
      learn it by keeping a record honest enough to count against yourself — and then counting.
      There is an automated check for it now, and I still break it.</p>
    </Disclosure>

    <!-- The operating principles, stated as consequences of the failures above
         rather than as a separate creed. Each one names the decision it changed. -->
    <div class="card p-5 mt-5">
      <div class="eyebrow mb-4">What those cost me, stated as rules</div>
      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-8 gap-y-3">
        {[
          ['Suspect the measurement before the thing measured', 'The one above, stated as a rule. An empty result is a question, not an answer.'],
          ['A green exit code is not success — read the counts', 'An indexer reported "completed" while processing 194 files into 0 entities.'],
          ['Verify with a query whose answer you already know', 'Every real defect this year surfaced this way. None came from a passing test.'],
          ['Report the running process, never the config', 'A dashboard read configuration and called a live service down. A wrong red light is worse than none.'],
        ].map(([rule, why]) => (
          <div class="border-l-2 border-neural/40 pl-3">
            <b class="text-[13px] block leading-snug">{rule}</b>
            <span class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-ghost">{why}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-ghost mt-4 mb-0 pt-3 border-t border-line/60">
        These are stored as queryable records rather than as a document, retrieved by describing the
        situation rather than naming the rule — which is what the research below measures. Ten of
        sixteen changed nothing when tested, and that result is in the paper too.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ── Code & public output ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <Section
    id="code"
    title="Code and public output"
    lede="What can be shown, and what cannot. The two production repositories carry the real work but are not mine to publish — one is client-owned, the other goes open source at release. What I can show now is the trail: how the work is documented and committed."
  >
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-3 mb-5">
      {repos.map((r, i) => (
        <div class="card p-5" style={`animation: countIn .5s ease-out ${i * 70}ms both`}>
          <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-1">
            <b class="text-[14px] text-synapse">{r.name}</b>
            <span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-fg-ghost border border-line rounded px-1.5 py-0.5 shrink-0">
              {r.state}
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="text-[13px] text-fg mb-2">{r.what}</div>
          <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-ghost m-0 flex-1">{r.detail}</p>
          {r.href && (
            <a href={r.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
               class="text-[12px] mt-3 inline-block no-underline hover:underline">View on GitHub →</a>
          )}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <!-- Two more that are public but live elsewhere, plus the one that is a
         running demo rather than a repository. -->
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-3 mt-3">
      <div class="card p-5 flex flex-col">
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-1">
          <b class="text-[14px] text-synapse">aisvet2 · aiworld</b>
          <span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-fg-ghost border border-line rounded px-1.5 py-0.5 shrink-0">
            gitlab · WIP
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="text-[13px] text-fg mb-2">The publishing side.</div>
        <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-ghost m-0 flex-1">
          The Slovak AI magazine and its English sibling, both public and both still moving.
          Hosted from GitLab Pages rather than here, which is why they are named and not linked
          as repositories.
        </p>
        <a href="http://aiworld.aisvet.sk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
           class="text-[12px] mt-3 inline-block no-underline hover:underline">aiworld.aisvet.sk →</a>
      </div>

      <div class="card p-5 flex flex-col md:col-span-2">
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-1">
          <b class="text-[14px] text-synapse">msg-ai — a 1B model on the front end</b>
          <span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-fg-ghost border border-line rounded px-1.5 py-0.5 shrink-0">
            live demo · unfinished
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="text-[13px] text-fg mb-2">
          A corporate site with a local model answering on it — not an API call to a provider.
        </div>
        <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-ghost m-0 flex-1">
          An experiment, never contracted: <b class="text-fg-dim">Gemma-3 1B deployed on the page's
          own front end</b>, running on the same 8&nbsp;GB VPS that carried Team Brain in
          production at the time. It never shipped — my role ended first — and it still answers,
          which makes it the honest artefact of that period: unfinished, working, and the first
          time I put a local model where an actual visitor could talk to it. The design system
          came out of Claude Design and Pencil.dev.
        </p>
        <a href="https://brain.msgweb.io/msg-ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
           class="text-[12px] mt-3 inline-block no-underline hover:underline">
          Open the demo — the assistant is behind the red dot, bottom right →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card p-5 mb-5">
      <div class="eyebrow mb-3">The trail, and why it counts</div>
      <p class="text-[13.5px] text-fg-dim m-0 mb-3">
        Both projects carry a long commit history against a written plan, with each finding
        recorded as it happened rather than reconstructed afterwards. That is not housekeeping —
        it is why the "what went wrong" section above could be written from records instead of
        memory, and why a mistake made in May is retrievable in August by describing the
        situation rather than remembering its name.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-fg-ghost m-0">
        Exploratory repositories — harnesses, experiments, things that were tried and abandoned —
        can be published alongside these, with the caveat they deserve: <b class="text-fg-dim">they
        are artefacts of learning, not production code.</b> The production work is best walked
        through live, where the history and the reasoning can be shown together.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4 p-5 card">
      <img src={`${base}aisvet-logo.jpg`} alt="AI Svet channel logo" width="72" height="72"
           class="rounded-xl shrink-0"
           style="box-shadow: 0 0 26px -6px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-neural) 65%, transparent)" />
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow mb-1.5">Explaining it in a language that has none of it</div>
        <p class="text-[13.5px] text-fg-dim m-0">
          Slovak has almost no technical material on AI. Everything below is written and recorded in
          it — agentic workflows, system design, agent harnesses, alignment at work — because the
          alternative is that the people who most need to understand these systems read a marketing
          summary of them instead.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-3">
      {public_output.map((o) => (
        <div class="card p-5 flex flex-col">
          <div class="text-[10.5px] tracking-[.14em] uppercase text-fg-ghost mb-2">{o.what}</div>
          <b class="text-[14px] block mb-1.5">{o.name}</b>
          <p class="text-[12.5px] text-fg-dim m-0 flex-1" set:html={o.detail} />
          {o.meta && (
            <div class="text-[11.5px] text-fg-ghost mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-line/50">{o.meta}</div>
          )}
          {o.href && (
            <a href={o.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
               class="text-[12.5px] mt-2 inline-block no-underline hover:underline">Visit →</a>
          )}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>
